# Grant read/write access to HackRF device nodes, and keep them awake.
#
# The distro hackrf package already ships 53-hackrf.rules, but it grants access
# with TAG+="uaccess" alone. That is an ACL logind hands to a user with an
# active local seat, so on a headless machine reached over SSH nothing is
# granted: the node stays root:root 0664, readable but not writable, and the
# radio cannot be opened. Symptom: "HackRF is present at /dev/bus/usb/... but
# could not be opened".
#
# The second job here is USB autosuspend. It is a known HackRF problem and the
# classic symptom is `hackrf_info` failing once and succeeding immediately
# afterwards. For a daemon that streams continuously it shows up differently
# and worse: a suspended device fails its next bulk transfer with "hardware
# fault or protocol violation", and because control transfers still answer, a
# naive recovery check thinks the radio is fine and restarts straight into the
# same failure. We watched exactly that on the XPS — 435 faults, 408 of them
# spaced at precisely the retry interval.
#
# This file supplements rather than replaces the packaged rule, so new device
# ids keep coming from the package. The higher number makes it win for MODE and
# GROUP. Change `wheel` to any group you belong to.
#
# Install as root:
#   cp deploy/60-hackrf-access.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   udevadm control --reload-rules && udevadm trigger
#
# On the aconfmgr-managed homelab this lives in roles/sdr.bash instead; see
# proxyd/README.md.
#
# NOT ENOUGH ON A LAPTOP RUNNING TLP. TLP applies its own USB power policy
# after udev and will turn autosuspend back on, so the device also has to be
# listed there:
#   /etc/tlp.d/10-hackrf.conf:  USB_DENYLIST="1d50:6089"
#   systemctl restart tlp
# (`USB_DENYLIST` since TLP 1.4; older versions call it `USB_BLACKLIST`.)
# Access. Left exactly as it was when it was proven to work, rather than having
# power management folded into the same lines where a mistake would take the
# permissions down with it.
ATTR{idVendor}=="1d50", ATTR{idProduct}=="6089", MODE="0660", GROUP="wheel"
ATTR{idVendor}=="1d50", ATTR{idProduct}=="604b", MODE="0660", GROUP="wheel"
ATTR{idVendor}=="1d50", ATTR{idProduct}=="cc15", MODE="0660", GROUP="wheel"
# NXP DFU bootloader, for firmware recovery
ATTR{idVendor}=="1fc9", ATTR{idProduct}=="000c", MODE="0660", GROUP="wheel"

# Power. Separate lines, matched on the USB device itself: `power/control` is
# reported to be unreliable when set from udev (systemd#14491), so use the
# autosuspend timer, which is the form the HackRF community documents.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="1d50", ATTR{idProduct}=="6089", ATTR{power/autosuspend}="-1"
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="1d50", ATTR{idProduct}=="604b", ATTR{power/autosuspend}="-1"
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="1d50", ATTR{idProduct}=="cc15", ATTR{power/autosuspend}="-1"
